PROPOSED LICENSE FOR STOCKSBREW (decision for the owner; not yet in force)
=========================================================================

Recommendation: GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later (AGPL-3.0-or-later)
for the repository, with packages/stocksbrew-cli optionally under MIT.

How to adopt it
---------------
1. Copy the canonical license text verbatim from
   https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.txt into a file named LICENSE.
   (Do not retype it; use the official text unchanged.)
2. Delete this file.
3. Add to package.json: "license": "AGPL-3.0-or-later" (and remove "private": true
   only if you intend to publish to npm; keeping it is fine for a web app).
4. Optional: put MIT text in packages/stocksbrew-cli/LICENSE and set its
   package.json "license": "MIT" so anyone can embed the client freely.
5. Optional: a short header in new source files:
   SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

Comparison for this project
---------------------------
stocksbrew is a hosted web app plus server-side jobs. The interesting question
is what happens when someone runs a modified copy as a service.

MIT
  + Shortest, most familiar, maximum adoption and reuse.
  - Anyone can take the whole product, run a closed hosted fork, and never
    share changes. No patent grant.
  Fit: good for the CLI/client package; weak for the app if you care about
  improvements coming back.

Apache-2.0
  + Permissive like MIT, plus an explicit patent grant and patent-retaliation
    clause, and a NOTICE mechanism for attribution.
  - Same closed-SaaS-fork outcome as MIT. Slightly more ceremony.
  Fit: the better permissive choice if you want companies to build on it.

AGPL-3.0
  + Copyleft that also covers network use: anyone who runs a modified
    stocksbrew for users must offer those users the source of their version.
    Keeps the project and its forks open. Common for self-hostable apps,
    including open-source finance trackers.
  - Some companies forbid AGPL code, so fewer corporate contributors or
    embedders. Contributors need to accept that their work stays AGPL.
  Fit: best match for "free and open source, and it stays that way".

Why AGPL here: the value is the whole running product, not a library. A
permissive license lets a well-funded clone take it closed overnight; AGPL
keeps forks honest without stopping anyone from self-hosting or contributing.
If you later want to dual-license or relicense, accept outside contributions
under a simple CLA or DCO sign-off (CONTRIBUTING.md asks for DCO sign-off).

What no code license covers: the data
-------------------------------------
The license applies to the code in this repository only. Data fetched from
providers (SEC EDGAR, House Clerk, Yahoo Finance, Alpha Vantage, Finnhub,
Marketaux, Twelve Data, OpenFIGI, scraped pages such as Finviz, Zacks,
StockAnalysis, Nasdaq, TradingView, CNN, Tickertape, Screener.in, and the
Reddit/Truth Social/Polymarket/Kalshi/CoinGecko APIs) stays under each
provider's terms. Many of those terms forbid redistribution, commercial use or
automated access. So:
  - ship no fetched data, caches, fixtures copied from providers, or database
    files in the repo;
  - say in the README that operators need their own keys and must follow each
    provider's terms;
  - consider disabling the scraping providers by default in the public build.
Third-party assets also keep their own licenses: fonts in assets/og (Instrument
Sans, Geist Mono, Inter: SIL Open Font License, include the OFL notices).
(public/agent-logos and public/trump-avatar.png were unused and have been
removed from the tree.)
